Free-form model text is a poor interface for a program that must choose a tool. Structured output does not make a model deterministic, but it moves failure into a boundary you can validate, measure, and handle.

## Model a decision, not a response

```ts
type AgentDecision =
  | { kind: "call_tool"; name: "search" | "lookup"; arguments: Record<string, string> }
  | { kind: "ask_user"; question: string }
  | { kind: "finish"; status: "success" | "cannot_complete"; summary: string };

function validateDecision(value: unknown): AgentDecision {
  // Validate enums, required fields, argument lengths, and allowed tools.
  return decisionSchema.parse(value);
}
```

Keep the schema close to the policy. Validate strings, ranges, IDs, and cross-field rules before execution. Tool implementations should validate again because a model boundary is not a security boundary.

## Preserve the rejected decision

When validation fails, record the schema error and model/version identifiers, then choose a bounded recovery: ask for clarification, retry with a changed context, or stop. Do not replace the invalid output with a guessed action. The rejected decision is often the most useful part of the trace when debugging.

This connects directly to [debugging AI agents in production](/blog/debug-ai-agents-in-production) and the layered [AI agent architecture](/blog/ai-agent-architecture). Both depend on separating intent, execution, and evidence.

## Test the boundary

Create fixtures for missing fields, extra fields, wrong enum values, oversized arguments, and valid-but-disallowed actions. Run them in CI and in an evaluation suite with real examples. Measure repair rate, stop rate, tool error rate, and user outcome; a high repair rate can hide a fragile prompt.

Structured outputs work best when the schema is small enough to explain, the tool contract is narrow, and every terminal state is explicit.
